Fix a soundness bug in let-such-that expressions whose condition depends on the allocation state (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/431). In a method or lemma, a condition could range over the allocated objects (`forall o: C :: ...`, a set, iset or map comprehension over a class, or a bound variable of a type parameter's type) or build a sequence with an initializer that reads the heap (`seq(n, f)`), without reading a field itself. The choice function of such an expression took no heap, so its axiom stated the condition about a fixed placeholder heap, and two such expressions evaluated before and after an allocation or a heap update proved `false`. The choice function now takes the heap whenever the translation of the condition mentions it. The same change removes a verifier crash ("undeclared identifier: $heap$old") for a let-such-that condition that wraps such a quantifier in `old(...)` or a labeled `old`, or that calls a two-state function. A condition that does not depend on the heap, for instance a quantifier bounded by a set, keeps its heap-independent choice function.
